theory_blocks.body:
**Ionising radiation** is radiation energetic enough to remove electrons from atoms, creating ions. It is not limited to radioactive samples brought in deliberately: a small amount of ionising radiation is present essentially everywhere, called **background radiation**.

Main sources of background radiation include:

- radon gas, which seeps out of certain rocks and soils (often the largest single contributor);
- rocks and building materials, many of which contain trace amounts of naturally radioactive elements;
- food and drink, which contain small amounts of naturally radioactive isotopes;
- cosmic rays arriving from space;
- smaller contributions from medical procedures and some industrial sources.

Because these sources are always present, a detector will register some count rate before any radioactive sample is deliberately brought near it.

